==English==

===Etymology 1===
Origin unknown.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|fadg|ing}}

# {{context|obsolete|intransitive}} To be [[suitable]] ({{term|with}} or {{term|to}} something).
#* Wycherley
#*: Well, Sir, how '''fadges''' the new design?
# {{context|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[agree]], to get along ({{term|with}}).
#* Milton
#*: They shall be made, spite of antipathy, to '''fadge''' together.
# {{context|obsolete|intransitive}} To get on well; to [[cope]], to [[thrive]].
#* '''1603''', {{w|John Florio}}, translating Michel de Montaigne, ''Essays'', II.17:
#*: I can never '''fadge''' well: for I am at such a stay, that except for health and life, there is nothing I will take the paines to fret my selfe about, or will purchase at so high a rate as to trouble my wits for it, or be constrained thereunto.
# {{context|Geordie}} To [[eat]] together.
# {{context|Yorkshire|of a horse}} To move with a [[gait]] between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

===Etymology 2===
Etymology uncertain.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{qualifier|Ulster}} Irish [[potato bread]] - flat [[farl]]s, griddle-baked. Often served fried.
# {{context|New Zealand}} A wool pack. traditionally made of jute now often synthetic.
# {{context|Geordie}} Small bread loaf or bun made with left-over dough.
# {{context|Yorkshire}} A [[gait]] of horses between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

